import
	../../html

intro-visibility html[]()
	- section "Private visibility",
		- p quote
			A private declaration is visible within the same module and nowhere else.
			{br}
			To mark any declaration as private, put a {keyword "-"} in front of the name.
			(It is not a part of the name.)

		- keen-runnable "user.keen", quote
			import
				./foo-bar

			main void()
				log foo-bar
				log foo # Won't work

		- keen-not-runnable "foo-bar.keen", quote
			foo-bar string()
				"\{foo\}\{bar\}"
			-foo string()
				"foo"
			-bar string()
				"bar"

		- p quote
			Functions, types, and specs can all be made private.
			{br}
			Even the individual fields of a record can be made private.

		- keen-runnable "record-user.keen", quote
			import
				./my-record

			main void()
				a r = ()
				log a.x
				log a.y # Won't work

		- keen-not-runnable "my-record.keen", quote
			r record
				x nat
				-y nat
			new r()
				0, 0

		- p quote
			By default, the implicit {fn-new} function for a record is as visible as the least-visible field.
			{br}
			That means that if there is a private field, {fn-new} defaults to private.
			If all fields are at the default visibility, so is {fn-new}.
		- p "It's possible to make {fn-new} private even when the fields are not:"
		- keen-runnable "record-user-2.keen", quote
			import
				./private-new-record

			main void()
				a r = ()
				log a.x, a.y # fields are not private
				_ r = 1, 2 # Won't work
		- keen-not-runnable "private-new-record.keen", quote
			r record -new
				x nat
				y nat
			new r()
				0, 0

	- section "Internal visibility",
		- p quote
			The default visibility is called {em "internal"}.
			{br}
			An internal declaration is visible if imported using a relative import path like {code "./foo"},
			but not if imported using a non-relative import path like {code "foo/bar"}.
			{br}
			(See {intro-link configs} for how to set up non-relative imports.)
		- p quote
			The assumption is that relative imports indicate a closer relationship between modules.
			{br}
			Non-relative imports are intended for libraries, which may want to hide implementation details.

	- section "Public visibility",
		- p quote
			To make a declaration visible to all importers, make it public with {keyword "+"}.

		- keen-not-runnable quote
			+my-public-function void()
				()

	- section "Explicit internal visibility",
		- p quote
			A declaration may be marked explicitly internal using {keyword "~"}.
			{br}
			This is rarely needed, except in these cases:
		- bullets
			- quote
				When defining a function named {function "-"}, {function "~"}, or {function "+"},
				a visibility mark is mandatory, since otherwise the function name looks like the visibility mark.
				{br}
				So you might declare:
				{keyword "~"}{sp}{function "-"}{sp}{type "my-type"}{lparen}{var-a}{sp}{type "my-type"}{rparen} .
				{br}
				This declares a function {function "-"} with internal ({keyword "~"}) visibility.
			- "You may want an internal field on a public record:"

		- keen-not-runnable quote
			+public-record record
				~internal-field bool

	- section "Ignoring visibility",
		- p quote
			If you don't want to deal with visibility,
			you can just leave everything at the default internal visibility.
			{br}
			This won't be a problem so long as you import your own code using relative imports.
		- p quote
			However, if you are writing a library for use by others, you should mark the public API with {keyword "+"}.
